# Copy to tools/machine.env and fill in. That file is git-ignored; this one is not.
#
# The split is the point: a mount point or a browser location is a property of one machine and has
# no business in a repository anyone can clone - it would be wrong on the next machine while looking
# perfectly right on this one. **Which keys exist is a property of the project**, though, and that is
# what this file carries. Without it the schema lived only in comments inside three separate tools,
# so a new machine had nothing to read.
#
# Everything here is optional. Each tool says what it does without the value: the copy to the test
# folder becomes a no-op, Chrome is looked for in the usual places, the port falls back to 8798.
# `tests/tools_test.py` reads the keys the tools actually use and fails if one is missing here, so
# this cannot fall behind the code.
#
#   tools/machine.py       reads it from Python (parses, never executes)
#   tools/totest.sh        sources it
#   python3 tools/machine.py   prints which keys this machine has set - the names, never the values

# Where the extensions are mirrored so the browser on the other machine can load them unpacked.
# `tools/totest.sh` writes <this>/crm and <this>/analytics, one direction, --delete per folder - so
# they are what Load unpacked is pointed at, and nothing else in that folder is touched.
# Unset: the copy does nothing, quietly, and tests/run.sh carries on.
ZOOST_TEST_DIR='/path/to/the/folder/the/browser/can/see'

# The browser every render, graph payload and headless probe goes through.
# Unset: the macOS location is tried, then google-chrome, google-chrome-stable, chromium,
# chromium-browser on the PATH. Set it only where Chrome is somewhere unusual.
CHROME='/path/to/google-chrome'

# The port tools/icons-receive.py serves the icon renderer on, for when something else on this
# machine already has it. Unset: 8798. The tool prints the URL either way.
ZOOST_ICONS_PORT='8798'
